<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Composes TWO UR5e arms (ros-humble-ur-description), each with a Robotiq
  2F-85 gripper (ros-humble-robotiq-description) at its tool flange, plus the
  ros2_control / gz_ros2_control wiring needed to drive all four in Gazebo
  Sim.

  NOTE: this file is written against the macro signatures published for
  ur_description and robotiq_description as of ROS2 Humble. Run
  `scripts/verify_description.sh` after installing/updating those packages
  (or just `xacro bartender.urdf.xacro | check_urdf /dev/stdin`) and fix
  any macro parameter mismatches it reveals; xacro macro APIs occasionally
  gain or rename parameters between package releases.

  ============ WHY ONE ARM IS PREFIXED AND THE OTHER IS NOT ============

  Arm A keeps the bare ur_description names - shoulder_pan_joint, tool0,
  base_link - and arm B gets everything under `b_`. That asymmetry is
  deliberate and it is the single decision this file most needs to justify,
  because the symmetric thing (a_/b_, or left_/right_) is what you would
  write from scratch.

  Renaming arm A would rename the planning frame, the end-effector link, the
  six arm joints and the gripper joint. Those strings are not confined to
  this package: they are the SRDF's group and every disable_collisions pair,
  both MoveIt controller lists, the controller_manager config,
  bartender_pour's ARM_JOINTS/EEF_LINK/PLANNING_FRAME/GRIPPER_JOINT, the
  tool-frame definitions in bartender_teach - and the taught points the user
  has already recorded and stored on disk, which are keyed by joint name and
  would silently become unloadable.

  Against that, the cost of the asymmetry is that you have to remember which
  arm is which. ur_description's own default for tf_prefix is "", so the
  unprefixed arm reads as "the original arm" rather than as an oversight,
  and every new file added for the second arm names both explicitly.

  If this ever grows a third arm, prefix everything then; the migration is
  the same size whenever it happens, and until then this keeps a working,
  tuned, tested pour pipeline working, tuned and tested.

  ============ WHERE ARM B IS ============

  Both arms hang off the same `world` link, which is what the ros_gz `create`
  call positions when it spawns this description - so `world` is NOT the
  Gazebo world origin, it is wherever arm A was spawned (see
  bartender_gazebo/launch/sim.launch.py: -0.45 -0.40 0.9, i.e. at one end of the bar
  top). Arm B's origin below is therefore
  relative to arm A's base, not to the world, and ARM_B_ORIGIN in
  bartender_open/layout.py has to agree with it. test_layout.py checks that
  they do.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="bartender_ur5e">

  <xacro:arg name="ur_type" default="ur5e"/>
  <!-- Gazebo Sim (Ignition), not Gazebo Classic. Passing both sim_gazebo
       and sim_ignition as true to ur_macro.xacro emits two <plugin> tags in
       the same <hardware> block, which gz_ros2_control then fails to
       resolve (it picks the first, classic one, which isn't registered). -->
  <xacro:arg name="sim_ignition" default="true"/>
  <xacro:arg name="use_fake_hardware" default="false"/>

  <!-- Arm B's base, in arm A's base frame. 1.06 down the bar and 0.80
       across, turned through pi so the two arms FACE EACH OTHER along the
       bar top with the bottle line running between them.

       That is a change. Arm B used to sit at 0.55 forward, 0.62 across and
       yawed -pi/2, on a pedestal beside a counter too small to carry it.
       The bar top is now big enough for both, and the yaw is what took the
       longest to settle, so it is worth saying why it is pi and not zero.

       Standing the two arms side by side, both facing the same way, was
       tried first: it makes the frames differ by a pure translation, which
       is tidy. It does not work. A UR5e carrying this gripper is not
       symmetric about its own centreline: the wrist hangs to one side, so
       with the fixed side-grasp tool orientation a target at negative y in
       the arm's frame is reached by swinging the shoulder a long way past
       the target's bearing. Parallel arms put the shared beer at positive y
       for one arm and negative y for the other, and measured, the negative
       one is the one that fails: arm B could not descend onto the cap.

       Facing them at each other flips arm B's y axis, so the beer between
       them is at +0.40 in BOTH frames and both arms work it from the same
       well conditioned pose. The measurements behind that are in
       bartender_open/layout.py under APPROACH_WINDOW. -->
  <xacro:property name="arm_b_xyz" value="1.06 0.80 0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="arm_b_rpy" value="0 0 3.141592653589793"/>

  <xacro:include filename="$(find ur_description)/urdf/ur_macro.xacro"/>
  <xacro:include filename="$(find robotiq_description)/urdf/robotiq_2f_85_macro.urdf.xacro"/>

  <link name="world"/>

  <!-- One arm + gripper. Called twice; everything that differs between the
       two arms is a parameter, so there is no second copy of the macro call
       to keep in sync. -->
  <xacro:macro name="bartender_arm" params="prefix ur_name gripper_name *origin">
    <xacro:ur_robot
      name="${ur_name}"
      tf_prefix="${prefix}"
      parent="world"
      ur_type="$(arg ur_type)"
      joint_limits_parameters_file="$(find ur_description)/config/$(arg ur_type)/joint_limits.yaml"
      kinematics_parameters_file="$(find ur_description)/config/$(arg ur_type)/default_kinematics.yaml"
      physical_parameters_file="$(find ur_description)/config/$(arg ur_type)/physical_parameters.yaml"
      visual_parameters_file="$(find ur_description)/config/$(arg ur_type)/visual_parameters.yaml"
      safety_limits="false"
      safety_pos_margin="0.15"
      safety_k_position="20"
      use_fake_hardware="$(arg use_fake_hardware)"
      fake_sensor_commands="false"
      sim_gazebo="false"
      sim_ignition="$(arg sim_ignition)"
      headless_mode="false"
    >
      <xacro:insert_block name="origin"/>
    </xacro:ur_robot>

    <xacro:robotiq_gripper
      name="${gripper_name}"
      prefix="${prefix}"
      parent="${prefix}tool0"
      use_fake_hardware="$(arg use_fake_hardware)"
      include_ros2_control="true"
      sim_ignition="$(arg sim_ignition)"
    >
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </xacro:robotiq_gripper>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- ============ Arm A: the pouring arm, unprefixed ============ -->
  <xacro:bartender_arm prefix="" ur_name="ur" gripper_name="RobotiqGripper">
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </xacro:bartender_arm>

  <!-- ============ Arm B: the opener arm, prefix b_ ============ -->
  <xacro:bartender_arm prefix="b_" ur_name="ur_b" gripper_name="RobotiqGripperB">
    <origin xyz="${arm_b_xyz}" rpy="${arm_b_rpy}"/>
  </xacro:bartender_arm>

  <!-- Gazebo cameras look along local +X. Pitching -90 degrees aligns that
       optical axis with tool0 +Z, the gripper's approach direction. -->
  <gazebo reference="tool0">
    <sensor name="arm_a_wrist_camera" type="camera">
      <pose>0.08 0 0.10 0 -1.5708 0</pose>
      <always_on>true</always_on>
      <update_rate>15</update_rate>
      <topic>/bartender/arm_a/wrist_camera/image_raw</topic>
      <camera>
        <horizontal_fov>1.047</horizontal_fov>
        <image>
          <width>320</width>
          <height>240</height>
          <format>R8G8B8</format>
        </image>
        <clip>
          <near>0.03</near>
          <far>5.0</far>
        </clip>
      </camera>
    </sensor>
    <sensor name="bartender_arm_a_wrist_camera_segmentation" type="segmentation">
      <pose>0.08 0 0.10 0 -1.5708 0</pose>
      <always_on>true</always_on>
      <update_rate>15</update_rate>
      <topic>/bartender/arm_a/wrist_camera/segmentation</topic>
      <camera>
        <segmentation_type>semantic</segmentation_type>
        <horizontal_fov>1.047</horizontal_fov>
        <image>
          <width>320</width>
          <height>240</height>
        </image>
        <clip>
          <near>0.03</near>
          <far>5.0</far>
        </clip>
      </camera>
    </sensor>
  </gazebo>

  <gazebo reference="b_tool0">
    <sensor name="arm_b_wrist_camera" type="camera">
      <pose>0.08 0 0.10 0 -1.5708 0</pose>
      <always_on>true</always_on>
      <update_rate>15</update_rate>
      <topic>/bartender/arm_b/wrist_camera/image_raw</topic>
      <camera>
        <horizontal_fov>1.047</horizontal_fov>
        <image>
          <width>320</width>
          <height>240</height>
          <format>R8G8B8</format>
        </image>
        <clip>
          <near>0.03</near>
          <far>5.0</far>
        </clip>
      </camera>
    </sensor>
  </gazebo>

  <!-- NOTE on gripper pads and friction. This file is NOT rendered with
       xacro directly; both sim.launch.py and move_group.launch.py run it
       through bartender_description/scripts/render_bartender_urdf.py, which
       widens the fingertip pads (and can groove them) and sets the pad
       friction. That has to happen outside xacro because it must REMOVE
       geometry that comes in from robotiq_description's macro. The script
       matches fingertips by name SUFFIX, so it treats both grippers alike.

       This used to carry a note claiming <gazebo reference> friction tags
       "do nothing" on this spawn path. That was wrong, and it is worth
       knowing why, because it wasted time. Two separate things are true:
         - the <surface> blocks robotiq_description puts INSIDE its
           <collision> elements really are dropped, because URDF has no such
           element and the parser ignores it;
         - <gazebo reference="link"><mu1> really does convert, verified with
           `ign sdf -p`, but ONLY onto collisions that have no name=
           attribute. Name them and the match fails silently, with no
           warning and no <mu> in the output.
       The original experiment swept mu during the old overhead NECK grasp,
       which held the bottle geometrically with the fingers wrapped around
       the neck, so friction could not have changed the result whatever the
       tags did. The null result was real; the conclusion drawn from it was
       not. -->

  <!-- ============ gz_ros2_control plugin ============ -->
  <!-- Only needed once for the whole model, however many arms it has:
       ur_macro.xacro's sim_ignition arg already adds a <ros2_control> block
       per arm and robotiq's macro one per gripper, and this single plugin
       declaration starts the one controller_manager that owns all four and
       points it at the shared controllers.yaml. -->
  <xacro:if value="$(arg sim_ignition)">
    <gazebo>
      <plugin filename="gz_ros2_control-system" name="gz_ros2_control::GazeboSimROS2ControlPlugin">
        <parameters>$(find bartender_description)/config/controllers.yaml</parameters>
      </plugin>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:if>

</robot>
